You're on .NET 10, so the best approach is to use the **`System.IO.Abstractions`** NuGet package rather than rolling a custom wrapper. It's battle-tested and covers `File`, `Directory`, `Path`, etc.

### 1. Install the packages

```bash
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers  # in your test project
```

### 2. Register in DI

```csharp
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IFileSystem, FileSystem>();
```

### 3. Inject `IFileSystem` into your classes

```csharp
public class ConfigService(IFileSystem fileSystem)
{
    public void Save(string path, string content)
        => fileSystem.File.WriteAllText(path, content);

    public string Load(string path)
        => fileSystem.File.ReadAllText(path);

    public bool OutputExists(string path)
        => fileSystem.Directory.Exists(path);
}
```

### 4. Test with `MockFileSystem`

```csharp
var mockFs = new MockFileSystem(new Dictionary<string, MockFileData>
{
    { "/data/config.json", new MockFileData("{\"key\": \"value\"}") }
});

var service = new ConfigService(mockFs);

Assert.Equal("{\"key\": \"value\"}", service.Load("/data/config.json"));
service.Save("/data/output.txt", "hello");
Assert.True(mockFs.File.Exists("/data/output.txt"));
```

### Why this over a custom wrapper?

| Custom `IFileWrapper` | `System.IO.Abstractions` |
|-|-|
| You maintain it | Community-maintained, complete |
| Only covers what you add | Covers all of `File`, `Directory`, `Path`, `Stream`, etc. |
| Easy to miss edge cases | Mirrors real `System.IO` API surface |

The key migration pattern: replace `File.` → `fileSystem.File.` and `Directory.` → `fileSystem.Directory.`. The method signatures are identical.

